  "enhanced_information": "The patient's symptoms of pruritus and jaundice, along with a history of ulcerative colitis and hepatosplenomegaly, suggest primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC). Standard medical consensus indicates that MRCP is the non-invasive diagnostic test of choice for PSC, as it can visualize bile duct strictures characteristic of the disease. While ERCP can also show bile duct strictures, it is more invasive and typically used for therapeutic purposes rather than initial diagnosis. Liver biopsy can show onion-skin fibrosis, but it is not the primary diagnostic tool for PSC. Ultrasound and contrast CT can identify hepatosplenomegaly and possible bile duct abnormalities, respectively, but are not definitive for PSC diagnosis. Therefore, MRCP is the most appropriate test to confirm the diagnosis of PSC in this patient.",
  "analysis": "The patient's symptoms of pruritus and jaundice, combined with a history of ulcerative colitis and hepatosplenomegaly, strongly suggest primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC). PSC is commonly associated with inflammatory bowel diseases like ulcerative colitis. The most appropriate diagnostic test for PSC is Magnetic Resonance Cholangiopancreatography (MRCP), as it is non-invasive and effectively visualizes the characteristic bile duct strictures of PSC. While Endoscopic Retrograde Cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) can also diagnose PSC, it is more invasive and typically reserved for therapeutic interventions. Liver biopsy, although it can show specific histological features like onion-skin fibrosis, is not the primary diagnostic tool. Ultrasound and contrast CT are not definitive for diagnosing PSC. Therefore, MRCP is the most suitable test to confirm the diagnosis of PSC in this patient.",
